"""Test 2 (memory road panel's merged test): a messy personal chat history generated by code, with the truth.

One user writes short chat messages to their AI over 22 months (2025-01-01 to 2026-10-31). Every message is a "note".
The generator knows, for every note, which person each mention refers to and which facts it states, so filing (who is
this about?) and answering can both be scored exactly. Nothing here calls a model.

What makes it messy (the panel's list, reports/Memory road panel 2026.md section 6):
  - people are mentioned by full name, by "my sister", by nickname, by first name with context ("Mira from work"),
    by first name alone (only when recent context makes it clear), by pronoun, and through another person
    ("Kofi's flatmate");
  - same first names (30 pairs) and near names (Joao / Joaquin, 20 pairs);
  - facts in several wordings (a job as "works as a pharmacist" or "fills prescriptions at the chemist's");
  - changes written as plain restatements (only order tells which is current), explicit changes and corrections;
    retractions; near-miss lures ("is thinking about getting a ferret");
  - 10 close people (family, partner, boss...) with about 300 notes each (fan effect);
  - dated events written with relative dates ("yesterday", "on Saturday"), for "when did I last..." questions;
  - 100 notes deleted at the end (each the only source of its fact);
  - held-out types: the test world adds 2 attribute types and 2 relation types the dev world never has, so prompts
    built on the dev world cannot be tuned to the test world's slots.

    python brain/messy_world.py --split dev     # prints counts and samples
    python brain/messy_world.py --split test
"""
